<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements (MIN-292).

  The hardened runtime is required for notarization, and by default it forbids
  exactly what V8 needs to run. The three keys below are the minimum WITHOUT WHICH
  CHROMIUM DOES NOT START once the app is signed — and the failure only occurs
  after signing, never during development.

  What is NOT here is a choice, not an omission:

  - App Sandbox: never. It would forbid precisely what this app exists for
    (§4 of the framing), and Apple requires it only for the App Store, which we
    are not targeting.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!-- MIN-356 — APNs address for the signed bundle. `production` matches the
         distributed Developer ID profile; an unsigned development binary does
         not attempt to register (desktop/src/main.ts). -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
    <string>production</string>
    <!-- V8 compiles just in time: without this, the first page does not render. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- V8's JIT writes to executable pages that the kernel has not validated.
         Same reason, one layer lower. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Electron uses DYLD environment variables to launch its child processes
         (renderer, GPU, utilities). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- VOICE DICTATION (MIN-294). Under the hardened runtime, the microphone is
         closed by the kernel, NOT by TCC: without this key, the signed app gets
         nothing and the request window does not even open — the refusal is silent,
         and only occurs once the app is packaged, never during development (the
         development shell runs under Electron.app, which already carries it).
         It goes with `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` from electron-builder.yml:
         the entitlement opens the door, the sentence explains why. If either is
         missing, nothing happens. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- THE LOCAL AGENT (MIN-293), and this is the most consequential entitlement
         in the file.

         The round launches `opencode`, a 144 MB native binary installed by
         `npm i` on the machine and **which we do not sign**. Under the hardened
         runtime, the kernel refuses to load code signed by someone else into a
         process signed by us: without this key, `opencode serve` does not start
         once the app is packaged — and, like the microphone before MIN-294, the
         failure NEVER occurs during development, where the shell runs under
         Electron.app.

         ⚠ WHAT IT OPENS, AND WHY YOU MUST READ THIS WHOLE BLOCK.

         Alone, it says “this process may load unvalidated code”.
         **Combined with `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables`,
         three lines above, it makes minddy a TCC inheritance vehicle**: a process
         launched from the app inherits its permissions (Documents, Desktop,
         Downloads, microphone), and `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` becomes a way to
         make any library load into a binary that carries them. The second key
         cannot be removed — Electron needs it for its own child processes
         (renderer, GPU) — so the combination is the cost of the package, not an
         accident to fix.

         What makes it tenable lies elsewhere, and does not fit in a plist: the
         round dies with the app (`before-quit` requests this, MIN-293), nothing it
         launches survives it (`run_background` is removed from the local path, the
         child registry finishes the job), and the harness itself is fingerprinted
         before every fork. Removing any of these three without removing this key
         would be the real defect.

         ⚠ It changes the republishing fingerprint (scripts/desktop-fingerprint.mjs,
         `PACKAGING_INPUTS`): republishing + re-notarization. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
